B.Dz=new A.cD(6,"Two ways to win","One threat gets blocked. Two threats at once cannot both be blocked, because the opponent only gets one move.",B.fg,B.DW,3,B.ak,"You already hold columns 3 and 5 on the bottom row. Filling column 4 between them makes a run of three with both ends open, and both ends are reachable right now. They block one; you take the other.")
B.De=new A.h5(4,"stopFork")
B.DL=s([0,2,0,4],t.t)
B.Ds=new A.cD(7,"Take the square they want","The move that would be strongest for your opponent is often the strongest move for you. Before playing, ask what you would do if it were their turn.",B.De,B.DL,3,B.ak,"If the teal diamonds get column 4, they hold three on the bottom row with both ends open and you cannot cover both. Taking that square first costs you nothing and removes the whole plan.")
B.Fs=s([2,2,6,3,6,3,4,4],t.t)
B.DR=s([1,5],t.t)
B.Dq=new A.cD(8,"Do not build their staircase","Your piece fills a square, and the square directly above it becomes available \u2014 to them. A move can be safe in itself and still hand over the game.",B.mp,B.Fs,null,B.DR,"The teal diamonds have three in a row on the second row, needing either end. Neither end is reachable yet. Play column 2 or column 6 and your own piece becomes the step they climb. Any other column is fine.")
B.FM=s([2,1,3,2,3,3,6,4,4,4],t.t)
B.Dp=new A.cD(9,"Diagonals are the ones you miss","Rows and columns are easy to scan. Diagonals cut across both and are the lines most games are actually lost to. Check them last, every single turn.",B.ff,B.FM,4,B.ak,"The teal diamonds run diagonally up from the bottom of column 2. Column 5 already holds three pieces, so their fourth square is reachable on their next turn. Dropping into column 5 takes it.")
B.Fr=s([0,4,1,4,2,4],t.t)
B.Dw=new A.cD(10,"Winning beats blocking","If you can finish, finish. A threat you never have to face is not a threat.",B.fe,B.Fr,3,B.ak,"The teal diamonds have three stacked in column 5 and it is tempting to block. But column 4 completes your bottom row now, and the game ends before they ever play. Check for your own win before you check for theirs.")
